,"severity_c":30.94285714285713,"tasmax":[32.3,32.0,31.8,27.5,28.7,31.7,32.6],"tasmin":[13.0,14.7,15.7,15.8,14.4,14.5,13.2]},{"level":"+2.0 °C","percentile":99,"chain":"SMHI-RCA-NORESM","model_year":3,"start":"07-10","severity_c":33.81428571428575,"tasmax":[32.9,34.8,35.9,36.2,35.3,32.0,29.6],"tasmin":[19.2,17.1,18.4,18.8,17.4,19.2,19.9]},{"level":"+3.0 °C","percentile":99,"chain":"CLMCOM-CCLM4-CCCMA","model_year":4,"start":"08-11","severity_c":36.371428571428496,"tasmax":[38.1,37.5,37.7,35.6,34.1,35.5,36.1],"tasmin":[19.6,20.4,19.6,19.5,17.8,17.3,18.6]}],"validation":{"recon_all_days":362,"recon_all_rmse_k":1.4,"recon_hot_days":46,"recon_hot_rmse_k":1.08,"ensemble":[{"level":"ref91-20","days_ge30":11.7,"days_ge35":0.8,"hurs_max":106.1},{"level":"gwl1.5","days_ge30":18.9,"days_ge35":2.5,"hurs_max":105.9},{"level":"gwl2.0","days_ge30":24.8,"days_ge35":4.3,"hurs_max":107.6},{"level":"gwl3.0","days_ge30":38.2,"days_ge35":10.7,"hurs_max":103.1}]},"rule":"7-day windows, target = percentile 95.0 of mean tasmax over all valid windows of the ensemble, chosen among windows starting 1 June to 25 August, closest to the target","inserted_rows":[4872,5039],"citation":"MeteoSwiss & ETH Zurich (2025): Climate CH2025 - Daily Datasets. Federal Office of Meteorology and Climatology MeteoSwiss, Zurich, https://doi.org/10.18751/climate/scenarios/ch2025/data/1.0/","sources":["data/canonical/climate/","docs/experiments/validation_reports/climate_scenarios.md","data/canonical/weather_klo_2023_hourly.csv"],"prose":{"intro":"Phase C asks what a warmer climate and cooling do to the district. The weather is the observed 2023 year at Kloten with the change of the Swiss CH2025 climate scenarios added for two global warming levels. A heat-wave week taken from the same scenarios can be inserted into the July test week. Everything on this page is constructed weather, not a forecast of a particular year.\n","construction":["The scenario data are daily values per climate-model chain: mean, lowest and highest temperature, solar radiation and humidity, for a reference period and for each warming level. For every month the middle value over all chains is compared with the reference, and that change is added to the observed 2023 hours: temperature by an amount that moves from the change of the daily low to the change of the daily high through the day, radiation and humidity by a ratio.\n","The 2023 sequence of days, and the shape of a typical day, are kept. So these are typical warmer years with the weather of 2023, not new weather sequences.\n","A heat wave is one seven-day spell chosen from the scenario chains at a stated rank among all seven-day spells of a level (the hottest one percent for the heat-wave variants). Its daily values are turned into hours with the observed shape of a hot 2023 day, and it replaces the July test week.\n"],"stats":"The annual mean temperature rises from 11.6 degrees in 2023 to 13.0 and 14.1 degrees at the two warming levels. Days whose hottest hour reaches thirty degrees go from 15 to 48, and days reaching thirty-five degrees from 2 to 12.\n","heatwave":"In the inserted week the mean temperature is 23.0 degrees with today's climate and 27.3 degrees at the high warming level, with a highest hour of 38.1 degrees; the observed 2023 week had a mean of 18.3 degrees.\n","validation":"To test the step from daily values to hours, every observed 2023 day was rebuilt from only its own lowest, highest and mean temperature with a typical-day shape that does not contain that day. The error is 1.4 K over all days and 1.1 K on hot days.\n","limits":["One station (Kloten) and one weather year as the base: there is no year-to-year variability and no change in how often weather types occur, except in the inserted spell.\n","The climate-model data are bias-adjusted model output. Their highest daily temperatures at Kloten are not credible in the far tail, so spells are chosen at a stated rank, never by the single highest value.\n","Heat waves are sequences from single model chains made hourly by a documented method; their radiation and humidity are only consistent in the daily mean.\n","The scenario radiation is global only; the split into direct and diffuse follows the 2023 relation and a clear-sky shape, not a measurement.\n","The milder spell variants are less hot than the hottest observed 2023 week, so they are shown only as a sensitivity.\n"],"citation_note":"The CH2025 data are open data of MeteoSwiss and ETH Zurich and are cited as required by their terms of use.\n"}}
